WHEN BYTES MEAN DATA AND WHEN BYTES MEAN INSTRUCTIONS.

A detailed English Major C architecture document.
Status: normative-draft semantics of CODE and DATA for a symbolic
ARMv7-A unprivileged T32 program. No parser or executable realization.
Bound by "Given a machine - objects and execution.english" and
"All and only these initial operations are permitted.english".
This document grants no additional operations.

PART ONE. DECLARE THE OBJECT, NOT ITS INTERPRETATION.

A byte is an eight-bit value stored at a byte-addressed location.
A sequence of bytes can be copied, compared, stored or loaded as data
without carrying any intrinsic language-level declaration of its
eventual meaning.

An object of four consecutive bytes does not inherently declare:
  - that the bytes form a signed thirty-two-bit integer;
  - that they form an unsigned thirty-two-bit integer;
  - that they form one or more encoded characters;
  - that they form a virtual address;
  - that they form one 32-bit T32 instruction;
  - that they form two 16-bit T32 instructions;
  - that they form data for a table branch;
  - that the machine is permitted to execute them.

Those interpretations result from the operation used, the current
instruction state, the selected data representation, and the
mapping/permission contract. A word-sized integer data load does
not ask the CPU to decode a T32 instruction; a T32 instruction
fetch does not ask the CPU to return an ordinary integer datum.

PART TWO. THE FOUR SEPARATE ROLES OF AN ADDRESS.

First, a DATA ADDRESS is used to read or write a datum of a
specified width and encoding.

Second, an INSTRUCTION ADDRESS is used in a permitted T32
instruction-fetch/decode path to obtain an instruction.

Third, a BRANCH TARGET VALUE is a numerical value used by a
branch to select a later instruction address. In the special
BX/BLX case its low bit includes an instruction-state tag and
is not part of the actual Thumb-aligned instruction address.

Fourth, a PC-RELATIVE BASE is the architectural address from
which an instruction computes a displacement. In common T32
cases it is the instruction's address plus four, but certain
literal and address-generation operations align it first.

Although these roles may sometimes be represented by the same
32-bit bit pattern, they are NOT interchangeable operations.

PART THREE. TWO WAYS OF LOOKING AT THE SAME FOUR BYTES.

Take an illustrative region beginning at virtual address
0x00004000 with four bytes in increasing address order:
0x00, 0xBF, 0x70, 0x47.

This example chooses little-endian instruction bytes,
subject to an actual target manifest that declares the
relevant encoding and memory interpretation.

If an authorized 32-bit little-endian data load reads
these four bytes from 0x00004000, the resulting
data bit pattern is hexadecimal 0x4770BF00.

If T32 instruction fetch begins at the halfword boundary
0x00004000, the first 16-bit instruction encoding is
0xBF00, corresponding to a T32 NOP in a compatible
target encoding. Sequential T32 fetch would then reach
0x00004002, where halfword 0x4770 is the encoding
commonly shown as BX LR.

Thus the same four bytes, with the same memory addresses,
can be one 32-bit datum or two different 16-bit instructions,
depending on which architectural operation uses them.

This document does not claim that a given process has
permission to execute from 0x00004000 or that the
contents of LR point to a safe destination. An NOP
followed by BX LR is NOT by itself a complete safely
callable function without a valid runtime entry state,
executable mapping and permitted return location.

A different choice of first execution address could
produce an entirely different decode. The bare CPU
does not store "these bytes were supposed to be code"
as a semantic tag for each instruction boundary.
A checked English program must carry or reconstruct
the intended instruction boundaries independently.

PART FOUR. A TABLE VALUE IS DATA USED BY CONTROL FLOW.

Given TBB or TBH, the CPU performs a data-memory
read for the table entry even though the overall
instruction is an instruction that transfers control.

The data value from that table does not become a new
instruction encoding. It is multiplied by two and
added to the architecture-defined PC-relative
branch base to produce the next instruction address.

This is a direct example of data-dependent control flow,
not a case of the table's own bytes becoming executable
machine instructions.

A compiler choosing a table branch must prove
that the index and target are within the declared
program's permitted sets. TBB/TBH themselves provide
no high-level array-bounds check merely by being
named "table" instructions.

PART FIVE. AN INTERPRETER AND A NATIVE CPU EXECUTE DIFFERENT THINGS.

Suppose a byte array contains encoding values for a
hypothetical instruction language invented by the
English Major C project.

An interpreter written for ARM may read those bytes
as ordinary data, test or dispatch on their values,
and perform ARM operations to implement the abstract
language's effects. The CPU is executing the
interpreter's actual ARM machine instructions.
The interpreted array is NOT thereby ARM machine code.

A native-code generator is different. It writes bytes
intended to be decoded directly as instructions by
the physical processor. Moving from generated data
bytes to CPU fetch of those bytes needs a separately
authorized execution transition and the operating
system's permissions and code visibility rules.

A source-code preprocessor is different again:
it consumes text and produces source or an intermediate
representation. Text enclosed in an explanatory
NOTE or WHY block is inert with respect to execution
under the proposed English Major C source rules.
Such a block may mention "branch", "register", or
any assembly mnemonic without granting the operation.

PART SIX. WHY MEMORY PERMISSIONS MUST BE A DECLARED PREMISE.

A virtual-memory page may be mapped readable,
writable, executable, or subject to other protections
and operating-system policies. The actual protection
policy must be established for the target process.

A normal data read from address A, a data write to
address A, and an instruction fetch from address A
may have different permission and fault behavior.
A declaration that address A contains instruction
bytes does not authorize fetching them.

No right to simultaneously hold an arbitrarily
writable and executable memory mapping is asserted.
No permission to remap a page, produce executable
code on an Android device, call a dynamic loader,
or change process privilege is implied by this
first architecture grant.

The operation grant excludes system calls, cache
maintenance, dynamic code generation and the OS
facilities required to make those operations safe
and visible on a real target. A later extension
must specify those operations explicitly.

PART SEVEN. PHYSICAL CACHES AND PREFETCH ARE NOT SOURCE DATA TYPES.

A virtual instruction fetch might be served by an
instruction cache. A data load might be served
by a data cache, other levels of cache, or memory.
Physical implementations differ. The English source
may not claim a specific DRAM-to-CPU bus event
for each architectural load, or that every source-level
instruction fetch is a separate physical DRAM read.

If software writes new instructions to an address
and later fetches that address, successful coherent
visibility depends on the architecture, cache maintenance
and synchronization protocol and OS permissions.
The English Major C draft does not yet grant these
facilities. The fact that a store returned does
not alone prove that later instruction fetches
observe the intended new instruction bytes.

Prefetching an instruction does not, on its own,
mean that the instruction is architecturally
executed. An incorrectly predicted path does not
become the program's chosen next instruction
address merely by having had bytes fetched.

PART EIGHT. ALTERNATIVE LANGUAGE DESIGNS CONSIDERED.

Alternative A: Make code and data one untyped
addressable collection and allow arbitrary casts
to and from instruction addresses.
Potential appeal: matches the superficial appearance
of ordinary machine memory.
Objection: hides execution permission, instruction
state, alignment, decode validity, and cache visibility.
Decision: NOT automatically permitted.

Alternative B: Keep a source-level distinction
between ordinary values and executable entry points,
while lowering both into machine-defined address
representations where allowed.
Potential appeal: explicitly captures validation
requirements and separates table data from code.
Objection: requires a genuine checked interpretation
and a way to represent indirect and computed branches.
Decision: candidate, NOT yet implemented.

Alternative C: Represent machine instruction bytes
as typed immutable data that a privileged assembler
or loader separately validates and publishes as
executable machine code.
Potential appeal: ties code publication to a
recorded validation and permission transition.
Objection: adds OS and cache-coherency boundaries,
and cannot be implemented under the current grant.
Decision: candidate for later extensions.

Alternative D: Interpret English Major C operations
without generating native machine code.
Potential appeal: can preserve rich long-form operation
descriptions, traced effects and explicit rejected cases
without treating author text as arbitrary native code.
Objection: interpreter correctness and its instruction
semantics still need separate tests. It executes
interpreter machine code, not ARM encodings simply
because they appear as English words.
Decision: possible initial runtime, not selected.

Alternative E: Use native assembly directly as
the executable ground truth and produce an
exhaustive English translation alongside it.
Potential appeal: matches the project's bottom-up
goal and preserves the original final-machine target.
Objection: translation must faithfully account for
EVERY executed operation and external boundary,
and cannot manufacture semantics missing from the
instruction manual or binary evidence.
Decision: selected initial EXPERIMENTAL METHOD,
not a complete compiler architecture.

No assertion about a future frontend to Idris,
preprocessor, interpreter, source grammar or native
compiler is established by choosing an experimental
method to inspect assembly.

PART NINE. CHECKABLE EXAMPLES AND FAILURES.

Positive case one: interpret bytes 00 BF 70 47 as
one 32-bit little-endian data word. Expect the
data bit pattern 0x4770BF00. No instruction
is executed by this data load.

Positive case two: starting T32 decoding at
0x4000, interpret the same bytes as two
consecutive 16-bit instructions. Expect the
instruction starting addresses 0x4000 and
0x4002, conditional on valid executable access.
No data load is implied by this instruction fetch.

Negative case one: reject a translation that
calls those bytes ONE 32-bit T32 instruction
solely because four bytes are contiguous.

Negative case two: reject a translation that
claims the branch table's byte value was
executed as machine code when TBB was used.

Negative case three: reject a native-code
execution claim based only on the existence
of generated bytes in ordinary writable memory.

Negative case four: reject a translation that
calls an interpreter's bytecode stream the
same thing as the CPU's native instruction stream.

Negative case five: reject an English program
which branches to bytes outside its verified
entry-point set even when those bytes happen
to be halfword-aligned and readable.

Negative case six: reject a physical-performance
claim deriving a particular instruction-cache
fetch or DRAM transaction from source execution
without any identified silicon or measurements.

These are REQUIRED FUTURE TESTS, not already
executed or independently accepted evidence.
